communityIssues.body:
### What feature would you like to add to the ONS Design System?

New documentation for the autosuggest component.

In order to link the JSON file to the autosuggest component locally, Alessio had to adapt the gulpfile.js file so that it was now as follows:
import gulp from 'gulp';
import definePrototypeKitGulpTasks from '@ons/prototype-kit/defineGulpTasks.js';

definePrototypeKitGulpTasks(gulp);

gulp.task('copy-static-files', () => {
    return gulp.src('./src/prototypes/example/data/**/*').pipe(gulp.dest('./build'));
  });

gulp.task('build-assets', gulp.series('copy-static-files'));


I then had to store the JSON files in /src/prototypes/example/data folder. This enabled the autosuggest JSON to be linked locally.

When it came to deploying the prototype, this did not work. I was advised by Richard McCarthy to create a new public git gist at https://gist.github.com/

Here I copied in the JSON file, named the file and added .json as the file extension. Once created, I clicked on the 'raw' button and copied the URL and then added this URL into the autosuggestData string. Once deployed, this worked.

### Why should this new feature be added to the Design System?

There is no guidance on the design system around linking the JSON to the autosuggest component either locally or when deployed. This is important information to enable autosuggests to be included in coded prototypes and used in user testing.

### Supporting material

_No response_

### Contacts

Alessio wrote the adaptions for gulpfile.js
Richard McCarthy recommended using Git Gist
Lauren Dowling - suggested this change

backlog.body:
We’ve got a use case where we want to style a link in red as a destructive / warning action. This is similar to the warning buttons pattern but where the thing is a link. Buttons aren't always appropriate or might dominate a UI - sometimes a link works better.
Screenshot of what we're implementing:

In this case it leads to a confirmation page.
What
It would be nice if this could be exposed as standard by the design system as a class - govuk-link--warning.
This would essentially apply this (suggested by @36degrees):
.app-link--warning {
  @include govuk-link-common;
  @include govuk-link-style-error;
}

Why
Styling this is a bit tricky as there's a bunch of things you need to set (link, hover, active, focus). It's not sufficient to just set the link to red. It would be nice to have a semantic class available - eg govuk-link--warning
This would also follow existing precedent in the design system for having semantic names for things - and mirror what we have for buttons.

ourIssues.body:
## Description of the issue

At fractional device-pixel ratios — browser zoom at 80/90/110%, or Windows
display scaling at 125%/150% (the default on most modern laptops) — the
selected radio's inner dot can render visibly off-centre inside the ring,
and asymmetric in itself. This was reported in #5104 and
alphagov/govuk-design-system#5812 and closed as browser pixel
rounding beyond the library's control.

That diagnosis is right, but the error has **two distinct sources in the
component's own CSS**, and both can be substantially reduced without giving
up the forced-colors behaviour that motivates the current implementation:

1. **Independent rounding of two boxes.** The dot (`::after`) is a second
  absolutely positioned box whose painted centre derives from *two* rounded
  quantities — `top/left: 12px` **plus** a `10px` border width. The ring
  (`::before`) rounds separately. At a fractional device-pixel ratio the two
  painted centres can disagree by up to ~1.5 device pixels.

2. **The disc is four border arcs.** Because the dot is drawn entirely from
  `border`, each side's width is rounded to device pixels independently, so
  the dot itself can rasterise lopsided (the "high left off centre"
  appearance described in backlog#296) — it is not merely mispositioned.
Proposed mitigation

- Anchor `::after` on the ring's exact centre and centre it with
  `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`. Transforms are computed in floating
  point, so this half-shift is immune to device-pixel snapping — the drift is
  bounded to ≤ ~0.5 device pixels instead of accumulating.
- Paint the dot as **one filled circle** (`width`/`height` + the
  `background: currentcolor` the rule already declares) so it rasterises as a
  single symmetric ellipse.
- Keep the border-drawn dot inside `@media (forced-colors: active)` — the
  border technique exists exactly for colour-override modes, and this
  preserves it there unchanged (the transform still centres the border box).

```scss
.govuk-radios__label::after {
  $radio-button-size: govuk-spacing(2);

  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  // Anchor the button's centre on the centre of the touch target; the
  // transform (floating point, immune to device-pixel snapping) centres
  // the button on that anchor.
  top: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  left: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  // One filled circle rasterises symmetrically at fractional zoom/DPI.
  width: $radio-button-size * 2;
  height: $radio-button-size * 2;
  border: 0 solid currentcolor;
  border-radius: 50%;
  opacity: 0;
  background: currentcolor;

  // Colour-override modes strip backgrounds; the border-drawn button
  // exists exactly for them, so restore it there.
  @media screen and (forced-colors: active), (-ms-high-contrast: active) {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border-width: $radio-button-size;
  }
}

// .govuk-radios--small variant
.govuk-radios__label::after {
  $radio-button-size: govuk-spacing(1);

  top: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  left: (($govuk-touch-target-size / 2) - $input-offset);
  width: $radio-button-size * 2;
  height: $radio-button-size * 2;

  @media screen and (forced-colors: active), (-ms-high-contrast: active) {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border-width: $radio-button-size;
  }
}
```

**Honest limits:** this is a mitigation, not a full fix. The ring's own 2px
border can still round unevenly at some scale factors, which no dot-side
change can address. But the dot can no longer be lopsided, and its residual
drift is bounded to about half a device pixel — in side-by-side testing the
difference is obvious. The checkboxes' tick shares the same
two-independent-boxes characteristic and could receive a similar treatment
as a follow-up.

I'm happy to raise a PR for this if the approach is acceptable.

## Steps to reproduce the issue

1. Use a display running at fractional scaling — e.g. a 4K monitor at
  Windows 125% display scaling (`devicePixelRatio` 1.25). Keep the browser
  at 100% zoom.
2. Open the Radios examples in the Design System:
  https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/radios/
3. Select options in the examples and look at the selected dots.

Because the device-pixel rounding depends on each element's fractional
position on the page, not every instance misrenders — scroll through the
examples (or resize the window slightly) and some dots will be off-centre or
lopsided while their neighbours look fine. On a standard-DPI screen the same
effect appears at 80/90/110% browser zoom.

## Actual vs expected behaviour

**Actual:** some selected dots render visibly off-centre within the ring
and/or asymmetric (thicker on one side).

<img width="776" height="324" alt="" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2520ad13-5905-444c-b4f7-cdfb939ef356" />

**Expected:** the dot stays visually concentric with the ring. With the
proposed change applied (we run it in production as a CSS override), the
same display renders every instance concentric:

<img width="776" height="323" alt="" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4dbf49db-525d-4795-bc02-43a579d1e739" />

## Environment (where applicable)

- Operating system: Windows 11, 4K display at 125% display scaling
  (devicePixelRatio 1.25; also reproduced on Windows 10 per #5104)
- Browser: Edge and Chrome, 100% zoom
- Browser version: FFox 153.0b10 (64-bit), Chrome Version 152.0.7943.0
- GOV.UK Frontend Version: 6.0.0

Hey,

I'm working on a project where we need to override some colours in the govuk components.

Button for example: https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/master/src/components/button/_button.scss

```scss
// _button.scss
$govuk-button-colour: #00823b;
$govuk-button-hover-colour: darken($govuk-button-colour, 5%);
$govuk-button-shadow-colour: darken($govuk-button-colour, 15%);
$govuk-button-text-colour: govuk-colour("white");
```

Since these variables don't have the default flag, to override the button colours we need to do:

```scss
@import 'govuk-frontend/components/button/button';

.govuk-button {
  background-color: $custom-color;
  box-shadow: 0 $button-shadow-size 0 $custom-shadow-color;
  // etc...
}
```

This is prone to human-error as we need to copy all class names and specific rules. Ideally, with default flags, we'd be able to do the following which is much safer:

```scss
$govuk-button-colour: $custom-color;
$govuk-button-hover-colour: $custom-color;
$govuk-button-shadow-colour: $custom-color;
$govuk-button-text-colour: $custom-color;

@import 'govuk-frontend/components/button/button';
```

I see default flags are set up for the global variables under `/settings`, but is there a reason they're not set up for component variables? If not, I'd be more than happy to submit a PR to change this.

Thanks!

