ourIssues.body:
When I started using the date-input I was expecting to be able to pass in a label param.

```nunjucks
{{ govukDateInput({
  id: "dob",
  namePrefix: "dob",
  label: "What is your date of birth?"
}) }}
```

In actual fact what I needed to pass was params.fieldset.legend.text:

```nunjucks
{{ govukDateInput({
  id: "dob",
  namePrefix: "dob",
  fieldset: {
    legend: {
      text: "What is your date of birth?"
    }
  }
}) }}
```

The actual usage seems overly complex given that I expect most users just want to specify a string.

I accept the fieldset/legend isn't really a label in the html sense, so would propose one of the following:

1. allow a new label or legend prop to be passed as a string, that, if set, is used instead of fieldset.legend.text

https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/e70f59d15a3cab9c949ef8f558d1eb49be7e4c91/src/components/date-input/template.njk#L90

becomes (written in JS/pseudocode as I am still learning nunjucks):

```
    legend: { text: params.label, ...params.fieldset.legend }
```

2. allow the fieldset param to optionally be passed in as a string

```
    legend: isString(params.fieldset) ? { text: params.fieldset } : params.fieldset.legend
```

This is low priority for me personally, but suggested in case others think it worthwhile.

## Description of the issue

At fractional device-pixel ratios — browser zoom at 80/90/110%, or Windows
display scaling at 125%/150% (the default on most modern laptops) — the
selected radio's inner dot can render visibly off-centre inside the ring,
and asymmetric in itself. This was reported in #5104 and
alphagov/govuk-design-system#5812 and closed as browser pixel
rounding beyond the library's control.

That diagnosis is right, but the error has **two distinct sources in the
component's own CSS**, and both can be substantially reduced without giving
up the forced-colors behaviour that motivates the current implementation:

1. **Independent rounding of two boxes.** The dot (`::after`) is a second
  absolutely positioned box whose painted centre derives from *two* rounded
  quantities — `top/left: 12px` **plus** a `10px` border width. The ring
  (`::before`) rounds separately. At a fractional device-pixel ratio the two
  painted centres can disagree by up to ~1.5 device pixels.

2. **The disc is four border arcs.** Because the dot is drawn entirely from
  `border`, each side's width is rounded to device pixels independently, so
  the dot itself can rasterise lopsided (the "high left off centre"
  appearance described in backlog#296) — it is not merely mispositioned.
Proposed mitigation

- Anchor `::after` on the ring's exact centre and centre it with
  `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`. Transforms are computed in floating
  point, so this half-shift is immune to device-pixel snapping — the drift is
  bounded to ≤ ~0.5 device pixels instead of accumulating.
- Paint the dot as **one filled circle** (`width`/`height` + the
  `background: currentcolor` the rule already declares) so it rasterises as a
  single symmetric ellipse.
- Keep the border-drawn dot inside `@media (forced-colors: active)` — the
  border technique exists exactly for colour-override modes, and this
  preserves it there unchanged (the transform still centres the border box).

```scss
.govuk-radios__label::after {
  $radio-button-size: govuk-spacing(2);

  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  // Anchor the button's centre on the centre of the touch target; the
  // transform (floating point, immune to device-pixel snapping) centres
  // the button on that anchor.
  top: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  left: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  // One filled circle rasterises symmetrically at fractional zoom/DPI.
  width: $radio-button-size * 2;
  height: $radio-button-size * 2;
  border: 0 solid currentcolor;
  border-radius: 50%;
  opacity: 0;
  background: currentcolor;

  // Colour-override modes strip backgrounds; the border-drawn button
  // exists exactly for them, so restore it there.
  @media screen and (forced-colors: active), (-ms-high-contrast: active) {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border-width: $radio-button-size;
  }
}

// .govuk-radios--small variant
.govuk-radios__label::after {
  $radio-button-size: govuk-spacing(1);

  top: ($govuk-touch-target-size / 2);
  left: (($govuk-touch-target-size / 2) - $input-offset);
  width: $radio-button-size * 2;
  height: $radio-button-size * 2;

  @media screen and (forced-colors: active), (-ms-high-contrast: active) {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border-width: $radio-button-size;
  }
}
```

**Honest limits:** this is a mitigation, not a full fix. The ring's own 2px
border can still round unevenly at some scale factors, which no dot-side
change can address. But the dot can no longer be lopsided, and its residual
drift is bounded to about half a device pixel — in side-by-side testing the
difference is obvious. The checkboxes' tick shares the same
two-independent-boxes characteristic and could receive a similar treatment
as a follow-up.

I'm happy to raise a PR for this if the approach is acceptable.

