and/or asymmetric (thicker on one side).

<img width="776" height="324" alt="" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2520ad13-5905-444c-b4f7-cdfb939ef356" />

**Expected:** the dot stays visually concentric with the ring. With the
proposed change applied (we run it in production as a CSS override), the
same display renders every instance concentric:

<img width="776" height="323" alt="" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4dbf49db-525d-4795-bc02-43a579d1e739" />

## Environment (where applicable)

- Operating system: Windows 11, 4K display at 125% display scaling
  (devicePixelRatio 1.25; also reproduced on Windows 10 per #5104)
- Browser: Edge and Chrome, 100% zoom
- Browser version: FFox 153.0b10 (64-bit), Chrome Version 152.0.7943.0
- GOV.UK Frontend Version: 6.0.0

The initial implementation of test fixtures does not handle `caller`, where a block of html can be passed through to a component. The suggestion was to add a `caller` param to the `fixtures.json` files as a top-level option (not something within the options object), e.g:

```
{
  "examples": [ 
    {
      "name": "default",
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "text": "What is your address?"
         }
      },
      "caller": "<p>Hello world</p>",
      "html": "<fieldset class=\"govuk-fieldset\">\n<legend class=\"govuk-fieldset__legend\">What is your address?</legend>\n<p>Hello World</p>\n</fieldset>"
    }
  ]
}
```

communityIssues.body:
## What

Rewrite the JS in the gem [to make use of the utility functions that are now importable](https://frontend.design-system.service.gov.uk/building-your-own-javascript-components/#building-your-own-javascript-components) in `govuk-frontend`.

### More details

- Use [Component](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/main/packages/govuk-frontend/src/govuk/component.mjs) or [ConfigurableComponent](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/main/packages/govuk-frontend/src/govuk/common/configuration.mjs) as a base class for the component modules
- Use [createAll](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/1cda5985d9c8cd277c047de1618a9572e70a3335/packages/govuk-frontend/src/govuk/init.mjs#L19) and remove the initialisation code within the components library

## Why

Previously the blocker to this would have been our browser support target being wider than `govuk-frontend` that seems to be changing. We include modern JS in `govuk_publishing_components` in several modules.

It seems the intent in GOV.UK Publishing Components is to follow the same conventions as those in `govuk-frontend`. Using the utility classes and functions that now are available would ensure that this is the case for our JS.

### Example implementation for `Component` and `ConfigurableComponent`

`Component` and `ConfigurableComponent` provide a consistent interface for components. Making the components consistent and following the same format will make them easier to maintain. It would also mean we could remove code already implemented by the parent class.

For example given a component:

```
<div module="gem-component" data-some-attribute="1" data-some-other-attribute="2"></div>
```

with

```
function GemComponent ($module) {
  this.$module = module
  this.someAttribute = this.$module.dataset.someAttribute

  if (!this.someAttribute) {
    throw Error('Did not include `someAttribute`')
  }

  this.someOtherAttribute = this.$module.dataset.someOtherAttribute
}

GemComponent.prototype.init = function () {
  alert(this.someAttribute)
}
```

Using `ConfigurableComponent` this would be


```
class GemComponent extends ConfigurableComponent {
  static moduleName = 'gem-component'

  constructor($module, config = {}) {
     super($module, config)

     alert(this.config.someAttribute)
  }
}
```

`ConfigurableComponent` [implements grabbing the dataset attributes of an object](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/1cda5985d9c8cd277c047de1618a9572e70a3335/packages/govuk-frontend/src/govuk/common/configuration.mjs#L63) and setting them to `this.config`. It supports attribute typing and also specifying if an attribute is required for initialisation too:

```
// within the GemComponent class 
static schema = Object.freeze({
  properties: {
    someAttribute: { type: 'number', required: true },
    someOtherAttribute: { type: 'string' }
  }
})
```

The base classes also implement checking if a component has already been initialised, an overloadable `isSupported` function and specifying a specific `tag` for `module`. If we use the base classes it also means that additional features will be inherited by our component JS in the gem.

#### Implementation of trackers

There are instances of GA4 trackers that are implemented in a similar way to components (using a `module`). I also think that `Component` and `ConfigurableComponent` could be used here too. A base `GA4Tracker` class could be created as an extension of `ConfigurableComponent` and we could make use of the `checkSupport` functionality as follows:

```
static checkSupport() {
  // Run the same checks as the `Component` class
  Component.checkSupport()

  const consentCookie = window.GOVUK.getConsentCookie()

  if (consentCookie && consentCookie.usage) return

  throw new Error('Cookies not accepted')
}

```

### Example implementation for `createAll`

`createAll` allows you to initialise specifically only the included modules (both from govuk-frontend and custom) in your application. We could remove `modules.js` as well as files we have in this format:

```
window.GOVUK = window.GOVUK || {}
window.GOVUK.Modules = window.GOVUK.Modules || {}
window.GOVUK.Modules.GovukCOMPONENT = window.GOVUKFrontend.COMPONENT
```

An example of it within an application that supports modules would be as follows:

```
import { CharacterCount, ErrorSummary, createAll } from 'govuk-frontend';
import { GemComponent } from 'app/assets/javascripts/govuk_publishing_components/components;

createAll(CharacterCount)
createAll(ErrorSummary)
createAll(GemComponent)
```

Might not be able to do this until we support module imports within our applications now I think about it. Would be worth exploring to see if is possible to use it though.

backlog.body:
What
Provide feedback to the user that a process is actively in progress and should be concluded shortly.

Why

There are no patterns for in progress indicators
Indicates that a process is actively in progress and should be finished in a relatively short time
Simple solution
Tested positively with users that something was actively happening
Testing demonstrated that our internal users expect more feedback when delays are encountered
There are no patterns for this feedback function
A progress bar indicator which is currently being tested (Beta) is used for dynamic, determinate
progress situations, we cannot provide determined (dynamic) progress for technical reasons.
Ellipsis natively implies that something is unfinished / incomplete
Animation created with CSS which degrades gracefully
Can be marked up semantically
Complies with WCAG guidelines
Can be given context and styled easily with preceding inline message
Is not bitmap based

Other components or patterns tried
There is a determinate progress bar pattern currently in testing however this cannot be used as we are unable to establish the length of processing time (only that the wait could be up to 5 mins)
Anything else
Research on this pattern
User need
When retrieving information from the store I need system feedback so that I know my request is in progress
After the user runs a search on a tax identifier, submission results are returned from short term storage. From these (instant metadata) results a linked original record could be retrieved from long term storage. This creates a break in the user journey which we need to bridge with system feedback. We identified 4 UI states from point of search results to the record being downloaded.
1 of 4 - Retrieval (metadata search results returned from short term storage - CTA to retrieve)
2 of 4 - In progress (Request sent to retrieve original submission from archive - up to 5 mins wait)
3 of 4 - Ready (Original submission ready for download)
4 of 4 - Downloaded (UI feedback showing which records have been downloaded)
Usually the user would not expect to separately action a retrieval request, then download once the file had been returned from archive. This cognitive disconnect caused us to experiment with an indeterminate progress indicator which helped the user understand what was happing and that a waiting time was normal.
State 2 and 3 have to be actioned independently otherwise we’d effectively be asking the browser to download a file without explicit user consent which is a security risk.
Usability testing Round 7 Findings and Usability research R8 in the Non-Repudiation Home space in Confluence.
Gaps
We observed in subsequent usability testing that the UI progress feedback reduced confusion and frustration regarding why a record had to be actively downloaded after waiting for it to be retrieved. It helped mitigate the extra stage in the retrieval journey.
We only prototyped with the animated ellipsis as a starting point however it is a simple and accessible solution to help resolve the need to provide in-progress system feedback.
Code
<div id="retrieving1" class="panel result-progress toggle1 show" aria-busy="true">
 <div class="info">
 <p class="event-record lede">Retrieval may take up to 5 minutes</p>
 <div class="busy" aria-hidden="true"><span>.</span><span>.</span><span>.</span>
</div>

.info {
  display:block;
  .event-record.lede {
    display:inline
  }
}

.busy {
  display:inline;
  span {
    font-weight:bold;
    margin-right:0.125em;
    animation-name: processing;
    animation-duration: 1.4s;
    animation-iteration-count: infinite;
    animation-fill-mode: both;
  }
  span:nth-child(2) {
    animation-delay: .2s;
  }
  span:nth-child(3) {
    animation-delay: .4s;
  }
}

For users with mobile accessibility preferences set to Reduce motion:
@media screen and (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { }
@media screen and (prefers-reduced-motion) { }

 .busy span {
   animation-play-state: paused;
 }

